I found several examples of using `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` with `McpServer`, `registerTool`, and `StdioServerTransport` in TypeScript. Here's what the search results show:

## Basic Example Pattern

A typical pattern creates an MCP server, registers a tool with `registerTool`, and connects it to a `StdioServerTransport`:

```typescript
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { z } from "zod";

// Create an MCP server
const server = new McpServer({
  name: "demo-server",
  version: "1.0.0"
});

// Add a tool using registerTool
server.registerTool("add", {
  title: "Addition Tool",
  description: "Add two numbers",
  inputSchema: {
    a: z.number(),
    b: z.number()
  }
}, async ({ a, b }) => ({
  content: [{ type: "text", text: String(a + b) }]
}));

// Start receiving messages on stdin and sending messages on stdout
const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
```

## registerTool Details

A typical registration with `registerTool` includes a tool name, configuration object with title and description, input/output schemas using Zod, and an async handler function.

## Important Note for Stdio Servers

When using stdio servers, do not write logs to stdout as stdout carries MCP's JSON-RPC; log to stderr or a file instead.

The search results show this is the current v2 pattern. If you need v1.x specific documentation, v1 documentation and v1-specific fixes live on the long-lived v1.x branch at the TypeScript SDK repository.


